            {
                "role": "user",
                "content": "What is gestational diabetes?",
                "sources": [],
                "timestamp": "2026-06-04 10:35:13"
            },
            {
                "role": "assistant",
                "content": "Gestational diabetes is a type of diabetes that is first diagnosed during pregnancy. It occurs when the body is not able to produce enough insulin, resulting in high blood sugar levels. According to the context, it is diagnosed based on blood glucose levels measured during the OGTT, and symptoms may or may not develop during pregnancy. Approximately 8 out of 100 pregnant women in the U.S develop gestational diabetes, and weight gain and changing hormones that occur during pregnancy can impair insulin function. This form of diabetes usually disappears after pregnancy, but women who have had gestational diabetes have a 40-60% chance of developing type 2 diabetes within 5 to 10 years.",
