            {
                "role": "user",
                "content": "What is gestational diabetes?",
                "sources": [],
                "timestamp": "2026-06-04 10:47:24"
            },
            {
                "role": "assistant",
                "content": "Gestational diabetes is a form of diabetes that is first diagnosed during pregnancy. It occurs when hormonal changes and weight gain during pregnancy impair insulin function, leading to high blood sugar levels. Symptoms may not always develop but are similar to type 2 diabetes when present. Diagnosis is typically confirmed through blood glucose testing during the oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT). It resolves after pregnancy in most cases, but women who experience it have a 40-60% risk of developing type 2 diabetes within 5–10 years. Risk factors include family history of diabetes, being overweight before pregnancy, high-risk ethnic groups, prior gestational diabetes, and a history of delivering a baby over 9 pounds.",
